meta {
  name: Update a person
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

put {
  url: {{host}}/api/persons/{{personId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Example Scope Admin, renamed",
    "email": "scope.admin@example.com"
  }
}

docs {
  UC-08. Any authenticated caller, subject to the handler's rules.

  roleId may only be changed by a System Admin; omit it entirely for a self-service name and email edit, which is what this body does.

  Changing your own email also needs "currentPassword", and "twoFactorCode" (an app or email code, or a recovery code) when your two-factor authentication is active (with only the Email method, use a recovery code: no endpoint sends an email code to a signed-in caller); either missing or wrong answers 401 "The password or code is incorrect.". An administrator changing someone else's email sends neither. Any email change revokes the person's tokens, so after changing your own, sign in again.

  An own email change mails the previous address, so it is charged to your account-email budget (one per HEIMDALL_ADMIN_EMAIL_COOLDOWN_IN_SECONDS, five per day-long window, shared with the verification resend and the two-factor setup code): 429 "An email of this kind was sent to you recently. Try again in a few minutes." inside the cooldown, 429 "You have reached the limit for account emails. Try again later." once the window's five are spent. Nothing changes then, and no recovery or email code is spent (an accepted authenticator code's time step is recorded, as for any accepted code). Both 429s carry Retry-After (seconds).

  While your email sign-in method is on (active, or a setup not yet confirmed), changing your own email answers 409 "Turn off the email sign-in method before changing your email address." after the credentials are accepted; turn it off, change the address, and set it up again there.
}
